{"abstract":"This paper introduces a model for analyzing the aesthetic value of game rules. Drawing on Nguyen's theory of agential aesthetics, this paper argues that the aesthetic value of a rule is related to the ways in which it contains modalities of agency. Using football's (soccer) offside rule as a case study, this article provides a way of thinking about the aesthetics of game rules.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Sicart, Miguel Angel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718839"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Game aesthetics","Game design","Gameplay","Sports","game rules"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the beautiful rule thinking the aesthetics of game rules","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"rem","url":"https://miguelsicart.net/publications/Sicart_Offside.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718839","identifier":[{"id":"85146510609","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-3401ee24-3a8f-46c7-9a3d-c50093508c75"],"keyword":["Game aesthetics","game design","game rules","gameplay","sports"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C1B5409D4EEE1D6C70D595D5CF792FE7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"rem","url":"https://miguelsicart.net/publications/Sicart_Offside.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design, Play, Culture and AI Section","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Miguel Angel","key":"sicartpau","last":"Sicart","locid":"689427b5-8d2a-44f3-b291-6db6ba598305","name":"Sicart, Miguel Angel","orcid":"0000-0002-8356-5394","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/15554120221149532","issn":["15554120","15554139"],"issn_org":["15554120"],"issue":"7","journal":"Games and Culture","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Games and Culture","title_org":"Games and Culture: A Journal of Interactive Media","volume":"18"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85146510609"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3401ee24-3a8f-46c7-9a3d-c50093508c75"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Beautiful Rule: Thinking the Aesthetics of Game Rules","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718839","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Whether we interact with video games or spreadsheets or social media, playing with software shapes every facet of our lives. In Playing Software, Miguel Sicart delves into why we play with computers, how that play shapes culture and society, and the threat posed by malefactors using play to weaponize everything from conspiracy theories to extractive capitalism. Starting from the controversial idea that software is an essential agent in the information age, Sicart considers our culture in general—and our way of thinking about and creating digital technology in particular—as a consequence of interacting with software's agency through play. As Sicart shows, playing shapes software agency. In turn, software shapes our agency as we adapt and relate to it through play. That play drives the creation of new cultural, social, and political forms. Sicart also reveals the role of make-believe in driving our playful engagement with the digital sphere. From there, he discusses the cybernetic theory of digital play and what we can learn from combining it with the idea that playfulness can mean pleasurable interaction with human and nonhuman agents inside the boundaries of a computational system. Finally, he critiques the instrumentalization of play as a tool wielded by platform capitalism.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Sicart, Miguel Angel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718907"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cybernetic Theory","Digital Play","Platform Capitalism","Playful Interaction","Software Agency"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"playing software homo ludens in computational culture","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-718907","ids":["itu-b3cab098-f24a-47cd-860a-b1528e75ce74"],"keyword":["Cybernetic Theory","Digital Play","Platform Capitalism","Playful Interaction","Software Agency"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-C905F6102FB1A3989E1ACC4CC5897C1E"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design, Play, Culture and AI Section","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Miguel Angel","key":"sicartpau","last":"Sicart","locid":"689427b5-8d2a-44f3-b291-6db6ba598305","name":"Sicart, Miguel Angel","orcid":"0000-0002-8356-5394","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9780262373173"],"isbn":["9780262047722"],"pages":"208","publisher":"MIT Press","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b3cab098-f24a-47cd-860a-b1528e75ce74"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Playing Software : Homo Ludens in Computational Culture","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718907","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"This research explores mediated ritual interactions in the form of pictures taken using a mobile device in tandem with two critical designs: Probably Not and World to GIF. We take our point of departure in Rich Ling’s understanding of mobile interaction as a ritual of social cohesion and social bonding to explore sociotechnical interactions with mobile technology. By means of playful critical interaction design methods, we explore how deviance from the ritual of mediated interaction through taking pictures on the mobile phone may enhance our understanding of artificial agents. Building upon Ling’s work, we mobilize the ritual aspect of mobile communication to open possibilities for imagining alternatives of seeing the world through artificial intelligence. We conclude by arguing that playful designs in mobile communication research may allow us to disassemble and reassemble mobile apps which enables critical reflection on the role of artificial agents in mobile rituals.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Neumayer, Christina","Sicart, Miguel"],"cuid":["S02-089.74-716291"],"filters":{"added":"2023-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Apps","Critical design","Play","mobile ritual","mobile visualities"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"probably not a game playing with the ai in the ritual of taking pictures on the mobile phone","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/363286444/Probably_not_a_game_CN_MS_accepted.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-089.74-716291","identifier":[{"id":"85153331300","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-1ee6786e-6817-48a1-82d6-7c98d845bf57","ku-70f9be57-3750-405b-a9f7-7a4172265f5c"],"keyword":["Apps","critical design","mobile ritual","mobile visualities","play"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-F2C7AEE973C18B56269B9F4C6C7C7BBF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"rem","url":"https://miguelsicart.net/publications/probably_not_paper.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/363286444/Probably_not_a_game_CN_MS_accepted.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design, Play, Culture and AI Section","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Christina","key":"neumayerpau","last":"Neumayer","locid":"15f1c085-b93f-40cc-9ef6-b8ae24783438","name":"Neumayer, Christina","orcid":"0000-0003-0450-2983","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Miguel Angel","key":"sicartpau","last":"Sicart","locid":"689427b5-8d2a-44f3-b291-6db6ba598305","name":"Sicart, Miguel Angel","orcid":"0000-0002-8356-5394","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/14614448231158654","issn":["14614448","14617315"],"issn_org":["14614448"],"issue":"4","journal":"New Media & Society","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"New Media & Society","volume":"25"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85153331300"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1ee6786e-6817-48a1-82d6-7c98d845bf57","ku-70f9be57-3750-405b-a9f7-7a4172265f5c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Probably not a game : Playing with the AI in the ritual of taking pictures on the mobile phone","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-089.74-716291","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
